{"purchaseId":"p2","currency":"GBP","amount":40.00,"usdAmount":50.00,"rateUsed":1.25,"rateAt":"2024-06-01T09:00:00Z","purchasedAt":"2024-06-01T10:16:00Z"}
{"purchaseId":"p3","currency":"EUR","amount":50.00,"usdAmount":56.00,"rateUsed":1.12,"rateAt":"2024-06-01T10:00:00Z","purchasedAt":"2024-06-01T10:17:00Z"}
```

Calling a rates service for every purchase adds network latency and an availability dependency, while the `KTable` provides a local state-store lookup without a per-record RPC. This non-windowed `KStream`–`KTable` join uses the rate currently held for the purchase's currency when the record is processed. If an old purchase is replayed after a newer rate update, it sees that newer current table value and can therefore produce a different result regardless of its original purchase timestamp. Historical billing can be made reproducible by retaining immutable, versioned rates and performing an as-of lookup using `purchasedAt` to select the rate version effective at that time.
